# Copy to .env for local dev. On Railway, set these as project variables instead.
PORT=8080
JWT_SECRET=change-me-to-a-long-random-string
ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me
# Optional: absolute path for the SQLite file. Defaults to ./data/queue.db.
# Persistence: everything (prompts, world-look reports, suggestions, seeds) is
# saved in this one DB file, and the app never wipes it on boot (migrations are
# additive). So locally, `npm start` restarts keep your data. On Railway's free
# tier the whole app disk resets on each deploy — to survive deploys, attach a
# persistent volume and point here, e.g.:
#   DB_PATH=/data/queue.db            (or)
#   RAILWAY_VOLUME_MOUNT_PATH=/data   (the app auto-joins <mount>/data/queue.db)
# Then confirm "dbOnVolume: true" in the deploy logs. This needs no code change.
DB_PATH=

# ── Free-provider catalogue (services/ai/catalog.js) ─────────────────────────
# All optional. A provider is only ever used once its key is set here — a
# missing key is a silent skip, never a fallback default, so this is how you
# control which free providers the router (services/ai/router.js) is allowed
# to fall through to when the Claude Code subscription is exhausted. None of
# these are required for the app to run; they only widen the "always be able
# to run a model" fallback chain.
#
# These keys feed the AI Router provider (question-mode chat / text generation
# only — no file editing). For IMPLEMENT-mode coding tasks on free models, see
# the OpenCode section below instead.
GROQ_API_KEY=
CEREBRAS_API_KEY=
GOOGLE_AI_STUDIO_API_KEY=
MISTRAL_API_KEY=
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
COHERE_API_KEY=
NVIDIA_NIM_API_KEY=
ZHIPU_API_KEY=

# ── OpenCode provider (free coding-agent execution) ──────────────────────────
# OpenCode (the opencode npm binary) is a full coding agent — it has its own
# agentic loop, file tools, bash, and session management. When you select
# "OpenCode" as the execution provider in the Dispatch Queue, the server
# spawns `opencode run` as a detached subprocess with the chosen model.
#
# CRITICAL: OpenCode reads its OWN provider credentials, independently of the
# catalogue keys above. The env vars below are passed through to the spawned
# OpenCode process (see services/providers/opencode.js#spawnEnv — it does NOT
# strip API keys, unlike the Claude Code provider). Set any keys for providers
# you want OpenCode to be able to use as backends:
#
#   OPENROUTER_API_KEY=     → Qwen3.8-Max, Qwen2.5-Coder (free :free variants available)
#   GOOGLE_AI_STUDIO_API_KEY= → Gemini Flash (free tier: ~1500 req/day)
#   GROQ_API_KEY=           → Llama-3.3-70b (free tier available)
#   ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=      → Claude (if you have free credits)
#   (OPENAI_API_KEY is NOT for OpenCode — see the paid gpt-4o lane below.)
#
# OpenCode ALSO provides built-in free models with NO key required:
#   opencode/deepseek-v4-flash-free  (tested — works for implement tasks)
#   opencode/big-pickle
#   opencode/nemotron-3-ultra-free
#   opencode/hy3-free, ling-3.0-tiny-free, mimo-v2.5-free,
#   opencode/nemotron-3.5-lightning-free
#
# Models are discovered automatically via `opencode models --verbose`
# (cached for 5 minutes). Free models (cost 0/0) sort to the top of the
# provider picker in the Dispatch Queue's New-prompt form.
#
# To use OpenCode on Railway, set OPENROUTER_API_KEY (or any of the above)
# as a project variable — the key flows to the spawned opencode process.
# No additional configuration is needed.
#
# ── Claude Code (subscription) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The Claude Code CLI is the default execution provider. On Railway, auth is
# via CLIAuth token (see CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN). The spawned env strips
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY to preserve subscription-billing (not metered API billing).
CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=

# ── Claude, second subscription (the app's own AI features) ───────────────────
# A token for a SECOND Claude subscription, from `claude setup-token` run while
# logged in as that account. It is read only by the local runner
# (scripts/queue-runner.js) on Antoine's Mac — set it in queue-server/.env there,
# never as a Railway variable.
#
# What it is for: the features pointed at the 'claude-side' provider in AI
# Settings (world-look, Idea Studio, suggestions, the Content navigator's text,
# and the chat on a task card) answer on this account, so they stop competing
# with the queue's coding work for the main subscription's quota.
#
# It is handed to one spawned CLI at a time as an environment extra. It must
# NEVER be written into this process's own environment: the main account's login
# is what every queue coding task uses, and overwriting it here would move that
# work onto the smaller subscription silently.
#
# Leaving this empty is fine — those jobs fall back to the main account and say
# so once in the runner log.
CLAUDE_SIDE_OAUTH_TOKEN=

# ── The second opinion on finished queue work (services/codeReviewPass.js) ────
# A queue task writes code with nobody watching and auto-ship publishes it, so
# the runner reads the change before it goes live: one review of the code, plus a
# security pass only when the change touches the login code, an endpoint, or adds
# something that looks like a key. Only two things can stop a change publishing —
# a secret committed, or a login check removed. Everything else is a note on the
# task card and the change still ships.
#
# It runs on CLAUDE_SIDE_OAUTH_TOKEN when that is set, so reviewing never eats the
# quota the queue needs for building. All three are optional.
#
# REVIEW_MODEL      sonnet by default. Not haiku: a review that misses the bug is
#                   worse than no review, and judgement is the whole value here.
# REVIEW_TIMEOUT_MS 180000 by default. A review that times out is simply skipped —
#                   it can never strand or block a finished task.
# REVIEW_DISABLED   set to 1 to turn the whole pass off.
REVIEW_MODEL=
REVIEW_TIMEOUT_MS=
REVIEW_DISABLED=

# ── Queue execution limits (services/taskRunner.js) ──────────────────────────
# Each queue task spawns a heavy agent CLI process. On a small Railway
# container, running several at once exhausts the memory allowance and the
# system OOM-kills a process (exit code 137) — the task dies mid-work. These
# three keep that from happening; all optional, defaults are the safe values.
#
#   MAX_CONCURRENT_WRITERS=1   # code tasks running at the same time
#   MAX_PARALLEL_QUESTIONS=1   # quick read-only questions at the same time
#   MIN_FREE_MEMORY_MB=300     # don't start any task with less than this much
#                              # container memory left (checked from the real
#                              # cgroup limit, not the host's free RAM)
#
# Only raise these on a host you know has headroom to spare.
MAX_CONCURRENT_WRITERS=
MAX_PARALLEL_QUESTIONS=
MIN_FREE_MEMORY_MB=

# ── TMDb film enrichment (services/filmEnrichment.js) ────────────────────────
# Free API key from https://www.themoviedb.org/settings/api (v3 key). Used to
# enrich film entities with synopsis/genres/keywords/country/cast. When unset,
# the enrich buttons simply report "key not configured" — nothing else breaks.
TMDB_API_KEY=

# ── GitHub discovery (services/codeDiscovery.js) ─────────────────────────────
# Optional. The "world's ideas" look and Building blocks search GitHub for real
# projects. Without a token GitHub allows only 10 searches/minute — with one
# (a free personal access token, no scopes needed) that rises to 30/minute.
# Create at https://github.com/settings/tokens — the search is read-only.
GITHUB_TOKEN=

# ── Slack ping when a task finishes (scripts/queue-runner.js) ────────────────
# An "Incoming Webhook" URL from Slack. The local runner posts one line per
# finished task (done or blocked, which engine ran it, how long, what it drew
# from the Claude subscription). Sent from the runner, not the server, because
# that is where tasks actually execute — so this needs no Railway variable.
# Leave empty to send nothing.
SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL=
# Optional: the link put in that message. Defaults to the Railway app URL.
APP_URL=

# ── Claude priority lane for the queue (scripts/queue-runner.js) ─────────────
# The queue runs on the Claude Code subscription first, then OpenCode Go, then
# free models. These guard the subscription so it can't be spent carelessly:
#   CLAUDE_QUEUE=0                    # turn the Claude lane off (all free)
#   CLAUDE_SESSION_RESERVE_PCT=85     # stop starting Claude runs past this
#                                     # much of the rolling 5h window
#   CLAUDE_WEEK_RESERVE_PCT=90        # same, for the weekly limit
#   CLAUDE_DEEP_WEEK_RESERVE_PCT=70   # stricter weekly reserve for deep (opus) work
CLAUDE_QUEUE=
CLAUDE_SESSION_RESERVE_PCT=
CLAUDE_WEEK_RESERVE_PCT=
CLAUDE_DEEP_WEEK_RESERVE_PCT=

# The second (smaller) subscription has its own, much later reserve — it exists to
# be spent, so holding it back as early as the main account would waste it. But the
# call that discovers a 100%-full window is a FAILED call, so it hands its work over
# to the main account a few percent early instead:
#   CLAUDE_SIDE_RESERVE_PCT=93        # hand over past this much of its 5h window
#   CLAUDE_SIDE_WEEK_RESERVE_PCT=97   # same, for its weekly limit
# The main account's own reserves above still apply to the handed-over job, so a
# nearly-spent big account can still decline it.
CLAUDE_SIDE_RESERVE_PCT=
CLAUDE_SIDE_WEEK_RESERVE_PCT=

# ── Real-money guard (services/billingGuard.js) ──────────────────────────────
# Nothing in this app may bill per token. Subscriptions (the Claude Code plan
# that runs the queue, the OpenCode plan) are fine — using them costs nothing
# extra. Pay-per-token paths (the Anthropic API fallback in claudeText.js, the
# chat tool loop in anthropicLoop.js, and any API key handed to a spawned CLI)
# REFUSE to run unless this is explicitly set to 1. Leave it empty.
ALLOW_METERED_API=
# Set to 0 to also skip the paid OpenCode Go models in the queue runner, leaving
# Claude (subscription) → free models only.
OPENCODE_GO=

# ─── The paid gpt-4o lane (Idea Studio conversations only) ────────────────────
# The ONE place this app spends real money, added deliberately: Antoine
# brainstorms best with gpt-4o, so Idea Studio conversations can run on OpenAI's
# paid API. Everything else — every build, judge, warmup, book, tag-lens and
# suggestion — stays on the subscriptions and free lanes it already used.
#
# It takes THREE things to switch on, and any one missing keeps it off:
#
#   OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...
#     The ordinary key that calls gpt-4o. Nothing spends without it.
#
#   ALLOW_OPENAI_STUDIO=1
#     The deliberate opt-in. Separate from ALLOW_METERED_API on purpose — that
#     one governs the Anthropic API fallback, and turning one on must never
#     quietly turn the other on.
#
#   OPENAI_ADMIN_KEY=sk-admin-...
#     A SEPARATE Admin key (only an org owner can create one; it can read cost
#     data but cannot call models). This is what holds the monthly ceiling
#     honest: Railway's free tier wipes the SQLite file on every redeploy, so a
#     local-only ledger would reset several times a month and let real spending
#     sail past the cap while the app showed green. With this key the app asks
#     OpenAI what it has actually spent.
#     A 404 from the cost API almost always means this is a normal key, not an
#     Admin key.
#
# The ceiling itself is a setting, not an env var:
# ai_settings.openai_month_cap_usd, default $10. Past it, Idea Studio falls back
# to the free lane and SAYS SO in the conversation.
#
# Also set a hard monthly budget limit on the OpenAI account itself. Any in-app
# cap assumes the app is the only thing spending on that key.
OPENAI_API_KEY=
OPENAI_ADMIN_KEY=
ALLOW_OPENAI_STUDIO=
